"# Serialization Result\n\n## 1. Verdict\n\n> ✅ **The history is serializable.**\n> **Equivalent serial schedule: T1 → T3** (T2 appears nowhere — it aborted).\n\n## 2. Justification via the Serialization Graph\n\nOver committed transactions only:\n\n| Component | Content |\n|---|---|\n| Nodes | T1, T3 (T2 excluded: aborted at t10 by first-committer-wins) |\n| Edges | T1 ──wr(A)──▶ T3 (T3 reads A₁), T1 ──wr(B)──▶ T3 (T3 reads B₁) |\n| Cycle check | Acyclic — topological order T1, T3 exists; no path returns to T1 |\n\nBy the **serialization theorem**: an acyclic precedence graph over committed transactions ⇔ the history is conflict-equivalent to some serial order. The graph's only topological order is **T1 before T3** — that is the equivalent serial schedule.\n\n## 3. Equivalence Proof (direct check)\n\nCompare the actual concurrent execution against the proposed serial order:\n\n| Property | Concurrent execution (committed ops) | Serial T1 → T3 |\n|---|---|---|\n| T1's reads | A₀, B₀ (snapshot v0) | A₀, B₀ (nothing has run yet) |\n| T1's writes | A₁, B₁ | A₁, B₁ |\n| T3's reads | A₁, B₁ (snapshot = T1's committed state) | A₁, B₁ |\n| **Final state** | **(A₁, B₁)** | **(A₁, B₁)** |\n| Reads-from relation | T1 → T3 on both items | T1 → T3 on both items |\n\nIdentical reads-from, identical writes, identical final database state. The concurrent history is conflict-equivalent to running T1 to completion, then T3. ∎\n\n## 4. Why the Result Depends on T2's Abort\n\nThe verdict holds **only because** the snapshot-isolation validator aborted T2:\n\n- **As executed (T2 aborted):** the dangerous conflicts (4× rw anti-dependencies, 2× ww between T1⇄T2) never enter the committed graph. What remains is the trivial acyclic chain T1 → T3. **Serializable.**\n- **Counterfactual (T2 commits):** the graph gains rw edges both ways on A and B — **cycle T1 →(rw A)→ T2 →(rw A)→ T1** (and the same on B). No serial order is equivalent: it would need T1<T2 *and* T2<T1. **Not serializable** — T2's updates on A and B would overwrite T1's (lost updates), and T3's reads would reflect that corrupted state.\n\nSo the precise statement is:\n\n| Claim | Status |\n|---|---|\n| History is serializable *as executed under SI* | ✅ Yes — equivalent to **T1 → T3** |\n| All three transactions committing would be serializable | ❌ No — rw cycle on A and B, lost updates |\n\n## 5. Bottom Line\n\n**Result: serializable, equivalent to the serial schedule T1 → T3, with T2 aborted.** The workload's total write overlap (WS(T1) = WS(T2) = {A, B}) makes it impossible for both writers to commit under snapshot isolation — the system serializes writers by force and keeps exactly one, which is why the surviving committed history reduces to a clean two-transaction chain."